@src 47:8
@pyq U.P. Lower Sub (Spl) (Pre) 2008
Artificial light can –
(a) Destroy chlorophyll
(b) Synthesise chlorophyll
(c) Bring about photosynthesis
(d) Not bring about photosynthesis
@ans (c)
@end

Chlorophyll absorbs mainly **red and blue** light and reflects green. Photosynthesis is **highest in red light**, then blue, and least in green. Bright **artificial light** (LED grow lights in vertical farms) works too. **Algae and phytoplankton** in the oceans produce about half or more of the world's oxygen, and fast-growing **Chlorella** converts sunlight to food very efficiently. *Cuscuta* is a parasite without chlorophyll.

@q 47:9-10

Photosynthesis **removes** CO_{2}; respiration, decay, burning and volcanoes add it.

@section 24.2 | Transport in Plants

@q 47:11-13

@table
Tissue | Carries | Direction | Cells
**Xylem** | **Water and minerals** | Upward from roots | Mostly **dead** (vessels, tracheids)
**Phloem** | **Food** (sugars, amino acids) made in leaves | Both ways (translocation) | **Living** (sieve tubes, companion cells)
@end

Water rises in tall trees mainly by the **transpiration pull** (cohesion-tension theory), helped by root pressure and capillarity.

@section 24.3 | Mineral Nutrition

@q 47:14-18

@table
Nutrient group | Elements
**Macronutrients** (primary) | **Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium**
Secondary macronutrients | **Calcium, magnesium, sulphur**
**Micronutrients** | **Iron, manganese, zinc, copper, boron, molybdenum, chlorine, nickel**
@end

Plants take nitrogen mostly as **nitrate (NO_{3}^{-})** and some as ammonium; they cannot use N_{2} gas directly. **Nitrogen deficiency** causes yellowing (chlorosis) of older leaves and stunted growth. **Sulphur** raises the **oil content** of mustard and other oilseeds. **Boron** is needed for nodule formation in legumes, pollen growth and sugar movement; **molybdenum** is part of the nitrogen-fixing enzyme.

@q 47:19-20

*Cuscuta* (amarbel) is a parasite that draws food from the host's **stem** through suckers (haustoria), not from its leaves. **Crop logging** is plant-tissue analysis during the season to judge fertiliser needs (developed for sugarcane in Hawaii).

@section 24.4 | Plant Hormones

@q 47:21-22

@src 47:23
@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Mains) 2016
Match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer from the code given below the lists.
@match
List-I (Plant hormone) | List-II (Function)
A. Auxin | 1. Cell elongation
B. Gibberellin | 2. Cell division
C. Cytokinin | 3. Growth inhibition
D. Abscisic acid | 4. Apical dominance
@endmatch
Code : A B C D
(a) 2 3 4 1
(b) 4 1 2 3
(c) 3 4 2 1
(d) 4 1 3 2
@ans (b)
@end

@table
Plant hormone | Main effects
**Auxin** (IAA) | **Apical dominance**, cell elongation, rooting of cuttings, phototropism (first plant hormone, Went 1928)
**Gibberellin** | **Stem elongation**, bolting, breaking seed **dormancy**, seedless fruits, bigger grapes
**Cytokinin** | **Cell division**, delays ageing of leaves
**Abscisic acid** (ABA) | **Stress / drought hormone**: closes stomata, causes dormancy and leaf fall; growth inhibitor
**Ethylene** | Gaseous hormone of **fruit ripening**; leaf and fruit fall
@end

@q 47:24-25

@qnote 47:25 | The source keys (d), statements 1 and 4. **Weeping (syneresis) of jelly** is linked to excess acid, and the source accepts **cytokinin** as a dormancy-breaking hormone, although **gibberellin** is the main one. Sugarcane's suited biofertilizer is **Acetobacter / Azotobacter**, not Rhizobium, and salt-water plants are **halophytes**, not thallophytes.

@q 47:26-27

@qnote 47:26 | The ripening gas is **ethylene** (C_{2}H_{4}); the source prints option (a) as "Ethane", a slip for ethylene, and keeps (a) as the key.

